---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { QualityChecksLab } from '../../../../components/english/QualityChecksLab'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import {
  QUALITY_CHECK_BUSINESS_DATE,
  QUALITY_RULE_COPY,
  buildQualityReference,
} from '../../../../features/english-quality/model'
import qualityStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-quality-checks.css?url'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'Data Quality Checks: What to Validate in a Pipeline | sql.sb'
const description =
  'What to validate in a data pipeline batch — identity, required fields, references, completeness, freshness and reconciliation — and when a failed check should block the release.'

const reference = buildQualityReference()
const rules = QUALITY_RULE_COPY
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="warehouse"
  styleUrls={[qualityStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'Data warehouse', key: 'warehouse' },
      { label: 'Data quality checks' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">Data warehouse · data reliability</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">
      Data quality checks: what to validate, and what failure should do
    </h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      A task can finish successfully and still produce data that must not be published. Quality
      checks are the executable rules that decide whether one batch, for one business date, is
      usable — and what happens when it is not.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      A data quality check is an executable rule over a specific dataset produced by a specific
      task: a table, a partition and a field or key. Checks are grouped by what they protect —
      identity, required fields, field semantics, references, expected set, freshness,
      reconciliation and format. A failed check does not always mean “stop everything”: failures
      that change shared business meaning block the release, while isolated bad records can be
      quarantined and the valid rows published with the alert kept open.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="scenario">
      <h2 id="scenario">One batch, eight checks, one release decision</h2>
      <p>
        The deposit balance snapshot for business date <code>{QUALITY_CHECK_BUSINESS_DATE}</code>{' '}
        runs every night. The task reports <code>success</code>, the SLA is met, and the row count
        looks plausible — but the batch can still be wrong in eight different ways. Each way breaks
        a different assumption that downstream reports rely on.
      </p>
      <QualityChecksLab client:load />
      <p>
        The lab reuses the same deterministic rule engine and banking fixture as the Chinese data
        quality course: every scenario injects exactly one fault, so the failing check is always
        traceable to the broken assumption.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="what">
      <h2 id="what">What to validate: eight checks that cover eight assumptions</h2>
      <p>
        A useful check list is not a random collection of SQL assertions. Each rule protects a
        specific downstream assumption, and each one answers a question a data engineer actually
        asks during an incident.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>The rule catalogue used by the lab</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Check</th>
              <th scope="col">Dimension</th>
              <th scope="col">Question it answers</th>
              <th scope="col">What it protects</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {rules.map((rule) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{rule.name}</td>
                <td>{rule.dimension}</td>
                <td>{rule.question}</td>
                <td>{rule.whatItProtects}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Row-level checks (identity, required fields, semantics, references) inspect individual
        records. Batch-level checks (expected set, freshness, reconciliation) inspect the whole
        partition or compare two layers. Both are needed: a batch can consist entirely of valid rows
        and still be missing 3,000 accounts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="where">
      <h2 id="where">Where in the pipeline each check belongs</h2>
      <p>
        Checks attached to the wrong stage either fire too late or drown in noise. The rule should
        live where its evidence first exists and where stopping is still cheap.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Typical check placement by stage</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Stage</th>
              <th scope="col">Checks that belong here</th>
              <th scope="col">What they catch</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Source arrival / staging</td>
              <td>File and record format; expected set by control table</td>
              <td>Unreadable payloads and missing deliveries before any transformation runs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Detail layer (DWD)</td>
              <td>Identity / grain, required fields, field semantics, referential integrity</td>
              <td>Broken rows that would contaminate every aggregate built on top of them</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Aggregate layer (DWS / ADS)</td>
              <td>
                Cross-layer reconciliation on the same scope; freshness of the represented date
              </td>
              <td>
                Layers that disagree with each other, and outputs that still represent yesterday
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Freshness is a good example. Checking whether the task arrived on time only proves the
        scheduler did its job; the check that matters compares <code>MAX(snapshot_date)</code> with
        the business date, so a green run over stale content still fails.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="failure">
      <h2 id="failure">What failure should do: block, or quarantine and continue</h2>
      <p>
        Not every failed check has the same consequence. A failed identity or reference check means
        the affected rows cannot be interpreted correctly, and any aggregate that includes them is
        wrong — the release must block. A failed format check on independent events can be handled
        by isolating the broken records without changing the meaning of the rest.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Release outcome per broken-batch scenario</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Broken batch</th>
              <th scope="col">Failing check</th>
              <th scope="col">Block on any failure</th>
              <th scope="col">Quarantine isolated records</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {reference.map((row) => (
              <tr data-tone={row.status === 'blocked' ? 'wrong' : undefined}>
                <td>{row.label}</td>
                <td>{row.failed.join('; ')}</td>
                <td>{row.status}</td>
                <td>{row.blockedUnderAnyPolicy ? 'blocked' : 'quarantined'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The table shows the decision boundary: the policy only changes the outcome for the isolated
        telemetry records. Every failure that touches shared business meaning — duplicates, missing
        fields, unknown branches, incomplete sets, stale dates, reconciling layers — stays blocked
        under both policies.
      </p>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="info">
        <strong>Quarantine requires evidence</strong>
        <p>
          Isolating records is only safe when their removal does not change the meaning of the
          remaining rows. Record which rows were quarantined, why, and keep the alert open until
          they are cleared or accepted.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="evidence">
      <h2 id="evidence">A check result is evidence, not a score</h2>
      <p>
        A useful check result carries the observed value, the expected value or threshold, the
        partition it ran against, the producing task, and a sample row when one exists. That is what
        makes an incident investigation possible later: without the partition and sample, “the check
        failed yesterday” cannot be turned into a fix.
      </p>
      <p>
        Warnings are not passes. A reconciliation drift below the failure threshold is still a
        signal that two layers disagree; if warnings are allowed to accumulate silently, the
        difference eventually crosses the threshold in production instead of during the run that
        introduced it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="boundaries">
      <h2 id="boundaries">How checks differ from contracts and observability</h2>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Three different questions that are easy to confuse</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Practice</th>
              <th scope="col">Question</th>
              <th scope="col">When it acts</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr data-tone="right">
              <td>
                <strong>Quality check</strong>
              </td>
              <td>Is this specific batch, for this partition, usable?</td>
              <td>During the run, before publication</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Data contract</strong>
              </td>
              <td>What did the producer promise the consumer, and how do changes get agreed?</td>
              <td>Before and during development, not only at runtime</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Observability</strong>
              </td>
              <td>Is the pipeline healthy over time: latency, coverage, error trends?</td>
              <td>Continuously, across many runs</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        A check can pass while the pipeline is unhealthy (slow but correct), and a healthy pipeline
        can still publish a batch that fails a check. Keep the three signals separate. Checks also
        tell you that recovery is needed — what to stop, replay and re-notify is covered in{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('pipelineFailureRecovery')}>pipeline failure recovery</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="tradeoffs">
      <h2 id="tradeoffs">Edge cases and trade-offs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Aggregate cost.</strong> A reconciliation check that re-aggregates a large scope
          is expensive. Run it on a stable scope (same date, branch, product, currency) and accept a
          bounded warning range instead of skipping it entirely.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>False positives.</strong> A rule with a bad threshold blocks a correct batch and
          destroys trust in the gate. Start strict on identity and references, and tune statistical
          thresholds with real history.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Partial batches.</strong> “Some valid rows and some broken rows” is a different
          problem from “too few rows”. The first can often be quarantined; the second usually
          cannot, because the missing rows are not identifiable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ownership.</strong> Every rule needs an owner who can decide whether a failure
          blocks a release, changes a threshold or is accepted. Unowned alerts get ignored.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Checks are not a substitute for design.</strong> If the source has no stable key
          or no control total, no runtime rule can tell whether the batch is complete.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="english-related" aria-labelledby="related">
      <h2 id="related">Related pages</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('pipelineFailureRecovery')}>Data pipeline failure recovery</a> —
          what to stop, replay and re-notify when bad data already moved downstream.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('lineageVsTaskDependency')}>Data lineage vs task dependency</a> —
          why a failed check needs the data graph, not only the scheduler graph, to find where it
          came from.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>How to make an ETL job idempotent</a> — why
          re-running the producing task after a failed check is safe only when the write is
          idempotent.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('grain')}>Data warehouse grain</a> — the row meaning that makes
          an identity check possible in the first place.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
